    "content": "In a significant political development, Somalia’s former National Intelligence and Security Agency (NISA) chief Fahad Yasin has publicly accused the Speaker of the lower house of parliament, Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur, of orchestrating a drone strike early Friday morning in Doolow, a town in the Gedo region near the border with Ethiopia. The attack, which occurred on Thursday night, was directed at a residence used by General Mahad Abdirahman Aden, who is also called General Shub. Reports indicate that Shub, who has previously led the Custodial Corps, was not injured in the assault.\n\nThe accusations come amidst heightened tensions between the federal government in Mogadishu and regional leaders, particularly in Jubaland, where Doolow is located. Yasin’s claim, made public through his social media channels, has intensified the political discord, as it directly implicates a high-ranking federal official in an act of violence within a semi-autonomous region. The Jubaland Ministry of Information has also issued statements regarding the incident, but they did not confirm or deny Yasin’s allegations, instead emphasizing the need for calm and federal accountability.\n\nGeneral Shub has been a figure of interest in Somali security circles. His role as former head of the Custodial Corps, which is responsible for guarding key government installations, places him within the intricate network of military leadership that has been reshuffled in recent years amid a power struggle between Mogadishu and regional states. The drone strike, if confirmed to be executed by international forces operating in Somalia, could suggest a coordination between local actors and foreign military partners, a claim that is often met with scrutiny.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this event underscores the fragility of political reconciliation and the potential for internal disputes to escalate into armed confrontations. The targeting of an opposition-aligned military figure, if proven, could destabilize ongoing efforts to secure the country and unite against threats like al-Shabaab. Many Somalis abroad closely monitor such incidents, as they often trigger clan-based reactions and influence remittance flows and investment confidence. The diaspora also benefits from transparent governance, and this accusation without a formal investigation could undermine public trust in institutions.\n\nThe situation in Gedo remains tense, with no immediate confirmation from African Union peacekeeping forces or the US Africa Command regarding their possible involvement in the strike. Calls for a transparent inquiry have been echoed by various civic groups, while the lack of official response from Speaker Jama’s office leaves the matter largely unverified. As the political fallout continues, the international community is closely observing how these accusations may affect Somalia’s electoral reforms and security agreements.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-fahad-yasin-links-speaker-jama-drone-strike-gen-shub/",
