    "summary": "Somalia's Federal Ministry of Information, Culture and Tourism has implemented a new regulation (Ministerial Decree No. 0001/26) to govern the registration and management of cultural and arts organizations in the country.",
    "content": "Mogadishu, Somalia — The Federal Ministry of Information, Culture and Tourism has announced the enactment of Ministerial Decree No. 0001/26, which establishes a provisional framework for the administration and registration of cultural and arts organizations in Somalia. The decree, signed by the Minister on July 27, 2026, applies to all entities and individuals operating in the cultural and artistic sectors, including local and foreign institutions.\n\nThe new regulation aims to implement the national cultural policy by streamlining the registration process for organizations, institutes, unions, councils, and individuals involved in cultural and artistic activities. According to the Ministry, the measure is designed to strengthen governance, promote transparency, and support the growth of Somalia's cultural sector. The decree provides guidelines for official recognition, operational compliance, and oversight.\n\nThis initiative comes as part of broader efforts by the federal government to formalize and support cultural industries, which have been largely informal due to years of conflict and instability. The Ministry emphasized that the registration process will help in organizing the sector, facilitating access to funding and resources, and ensuring that cultural heritage is preserved and promoted effectively.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this regulation holds particular relevance. Many diaspora artists and cultural organizations have been active in supporting cultural preservation and exchange between Somalia and their host countries. Official registration could enable smoother collaboration with institutions in Somalia, access to government-backed programs, and a clearer legal standing for their operations in the homeland. It also signals a commitment to rebuilding institutional capacity in the cultural sector, which diaspora members have long advocated for.\n\nThe Ministry has urged all relevant parties to comply with the new procedures and to initiate registration in a timely manner. Further details on registration requirements and deadlines are expected to be published in the coming weeks.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/05/dowladda-soomaaliya-oo-soo-saartay-habraac-cusub-oo-lagu-diiwaangelinayo-hayadaha-dhaqanka-iyo-farshaxanka/",
